// A `match` that falls through, uncaught. The fifth thing that was four.
//
// Until v0.1.470 the same program answered four ways. The interpreter raised
// `no matching arm in match` and pointed at the line. The C and LLVM backends
// ran a bare `abort()`: exit 134, no output at all. Wasm executed
// `unreachable`: exit 1, no output at all. And RV32IM fell off the end of the
// arm chain with the saved stack pointer still in a0, so the match evaluated to
// a STACK ADDRESS and the program carried on with it — the only one of the four
// that really did invent a value.
//
// Nothing compared them. This section of the harness compares exit status,
// stdout and the failure message for programs the interpreter refuses, and no
// program in it reached a fallthrough — the same gap `exit` sat in until
// v0.1.434 (Q-114), where the whole builtin was outside what the suite ran.
//
// THE VEHICLE IS AN INT SCRUTINEE ON PURPOSE. A missing case the checker can
// NAME is a compile error since v0.1.468, so a program that reaches a
// fallthrough is one the compiler refuses — except here: an int with no
// wildcard arm is a warning, because that arm of the checker is an admitted
// approximation and cannot name what is missing. So this file compiles on every
// backend with no flag, and it stays that way as long as that decision does.
//
// Execution mode: 4 backends, compared on exit status + stdout + failure message

let name = fn (n: int) ->
  match n with
  | 0 -> "zero"
  | 1 -> "one";
let _ = print (name 0);
let _ = print (name 2);
let _ = print "unreachable";
0
